{"title":"Adding fractions with different denominators","key_idea":"To add fractions with different bottoms, you must first make the bottoms the same using a common multiple.","method":["Find the least common multiple of both denominators.","Rewrite each fraction so both have that common denominator.","Add the numerators and keep the denominator the same."],"worked_example":{"problem":"Add 2/5 and 1/10.","steps":["The denominators are 5 and 10; their least common multiple is 10.","Rewrite 2/5 as 4/10 because 2 × 2 = 4 and 5 × 2 = 10.","Now add: 4/10 + 1/10 = 5/10.","Simplify 5/10 to 1/2 by dividing both top and bottom by 5."],"answer":"1/2"},"key_terms":[{"term":"common denominator","meaning":"a number that is a multiple of both denominators"},{"term":"least common multiple","meaning":"the smallest number that both numbers divide into evenly"}],"common_mistakes":["Adding the denominators instead of keeping them the same: writing 3/4 + 1/8 = 4/12 is wrong.","Forgetting to multiply the numerator when you multiply the denominator to make a common denominator."],"remember":"Never add the denominators; always make them equal first, then add only the numerators.","quick_check":[{"q":"Add 1/6 and 1/3. What is the least common multiple of 6 and 3?","a":"6"},{"q":"What is 1/6 + 1/3 after you rewrite both with the same denominator?","a":"1/2"}],"tell_your_child":"When the bottoms are different, we cannot just add the tops. First, we find a number that both bottoms divide into, and rewrite the fractions so they match on the bottom."}
